Undifferentiated Motor Weakness
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Subjective complaint of strength loss | Strictly normal objective motor strength grading (5/5) | Normal deep tendon reflexes and sensory exam

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-036
Reason for Visit: Acute severe distress due to perceived failure of muscle strength and inability to mobilize, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude an acute myopathy or evolving myelopathy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate a sudden subjective inability to walk and profound physical weakness. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out Guillain-Barré syndrome, acute transverse myelitis, or an acute myasthenic crisis, preventing catastrophic respiratory or motor failure prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My legs and arms just won't work properly, they feel like dead weight, and I cannot even lift myself out of bed.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden, intense subjective feeling of reduced muscle strength. The weakness has a generalized and non-anatomical distribution, affecting both upper and lower extremities vaguely. The patient admits to mild concurrent fatigue and severe emotional stress over the past week. There is an absolute lack of bulbar symptoms (no difficulty swallowing or speaking).
Functional Status: Currently refusing to weight-bear due to subjective weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute anxiety regarding neurological collapse.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 mmHg | HR: 86 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious, lying supine, but is in no acute respiratory distress. An unrelated recent lab report for a minor respiratory illness incidentally noted generalized malaise, a low-grade fever, and elevated inflammatory markers, all of which have since resolved and are unconnected to tonight's neurological presentation.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive neurological examination reveals give-way or variable effort during formal manual muscle testing, making objective strength grading difficult but indicating underlying preservation of power. There is normal muscle bulk without pronounced atrophy. There is a complete absence of muscle fasciculations. Deep tendon reflexes are 2+ and globally intact. Normal sensory examination across all dermatomes. Once coaxed to stand with assistance, the patient demonstrates intact coordination and normal gait speed despite subjective complaints of heaviness.
Environmental Observations: Safe, clutter-free environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no regular psychotropic or myopathic medications (e.g., high-dose statins).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Undifferentiated Motor Weakness - Status: Acute functional exacerbation, heavily influenced by underlying somatic anxiety.
Differentials:

Guillain-Barré Syndrome - Ruled out definitively due to intact deep tendon reflexes, lack of ascending paralysis, and normal sensory exam.

Acute Transverse Myelitis - Ruled out due to the absence of a distinct sensory level, intact sphincter tone, and lack of true upper motor neuron signs.

Acute Myositis - Ruled out clinically due to the complete lack of extreme muscle tenderness on deep palpation and normal urine color (no myoglobinuria).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s sudden inability to weight-bear mandated immediate after-hours evaluation to exclude rapid, life-threatening neurological demyelination or acute myopathy. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the critical risk of an evolving ascending paralysis. A meticulous physical examination confirmed perfectly intact reflexes, normal sensory pathways, and "give-way" weakness indicative of functional overlay rather than true structural nerve or muscle failure. By differentiating this benign functional weakness from an acute neuromuscular emergency, the clinician provided immediate psychological stabilization and safely averted a highly traumatizing, unnecessary emergency department admission for invasive neurological testing.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the structural integrity of the nervous system. The expected investigation results include normal serum creatine kinase levels and normal electromyography (EMG) studies, which will assist in confirming the functional diagnosis if symptoms persist.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rebuild physical confidence, set a strict routine of completing one full circuit of the living room immediately upon waking every morning; establishing this small, successful physical task will retrain your brain to trust your muscle strength and reduce somatic anxiety.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for a formal psychological assessment and potential physical therapy referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops genuine difficulty swallowing, becomes unable to speak clearly, or develops true loss of bladder or bowel control.

